WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.455
As a database engineer,

2
00:00:01.455 --> 00:00:03.900
you'll very often come across
columns in a table that are

3
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.645
filled with duplicates of
data and multiple values.

4
00:00:06.645 --> 00:00:08.820
This can make it quite
challenging to view,

5
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.800
search and sort your data.

6
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.950
But with the correct
implementation of normalization,

7
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.360
this challenge can
be dealt with.

8
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.740
By the end of this video,

9
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.180
you'll be able to
demonstrate how to

10
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.685
design a database in
first normal form.

11
00:00:20.685 --> 00:00:23.970
Identify the atomicity rule
and how to enforce it,

12
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.130
and analyze effective
ways to eliminate

13
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.295
the repeating group of
data problems in datasets.

14
00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.960
As you might already know from

15
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.595
previous videos in this lesson,

16
00:00:32.595 --> 00:00:35.400
the normalization process
makes it easier and more

17
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.850
efficient for engineers to
perform basic database tasks.

18
00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.910
It's an especially
useful process for

19
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.785
helping to fix the
well-known insert,

20
00:00:42.785 --> 00:00:44.990
delete, and update anomalies.

21
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:48.050
However, in order to achieve
database normalization,

22
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.340
you first need to perform

23
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.090
the three fundamental
normalization forms.

24
00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.110
The database normalization forms

25
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.215
include first
normal form or 1NF,

26
00:00:57.215 --> 00:01:02.615
second normal form or 2NF and
third normal form or 3NF.

27
00:01:02.615 --> 00:01:05.810
This video focuses on
designing a database to meet

28
00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.975
the first normal
form or 1NF rules.

29
00:01:08.975 --> 00:01:12.275
These rules enforce data
atomicity and eliminate

30
00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:15.965
unnecessary repeating groups
of data in database tables.

31
00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:18.950
Data atomicity means
that there must only be

32
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.480
one single instance value of

33
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.300
the column attribute in
any field of the table.

34
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.280
In other words, your tables

35
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.590
should only have one
value per field.

36
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.200
By eliminating repeating
groups of data,

37
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.745
you can avoid repeating data

38
00:01:31.745 --> 00:01:33.934
unnecessarily in the database.

39
00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:36.380
Instances of repeated data can

40
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.080
cause data redundancy
and inconsistency.

41
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.920
To understand this better,
let's explore an example,

42
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.280
to demonstrate data atomicity.

43
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.580
I've built an
unnormalized table called

44
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.055
Course Table within
a college database.

45
00:01:49.055 --> 00:01:50.540
It includes information about

46
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.250
the colleges computing courses,

47
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.690
along with the names and contact

48
00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.550
details of the course tutors.

49
00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:59.455
The course ID column serves
as the table's primary key.

50
00:01:59.455 --> 00:02:01.295
However, there are
multiple values in

51
00:02:01.295 --> 00:02:03.425
each row of the
contact number column.

52
00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:06.650
Each row contains two contact
details for each tutor,

53
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.500
a cell phone number,
and a landline number.

54
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.770
This table isn't in 1NF.

55
00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.850
It violates the
atomicity rule by

56
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.340
including multiple values
in a single field.

57
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.840
I can try and fix this by

58
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.730
creating a new row
for each number.

59
00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.505
This solves my data
atomicity problem.

60
00:02:22.505 --> 00:02:25.640
The table now has just
one value in each field.

61
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.550
But this solution has also
created another problem.

62
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.935
The primary key is
no longer unique

63
00:02:30.935 --> 00:02:34.095
because multiple rows now
has the same course ID.

64
00:02:34.095 --> 00:02:36.110
Another way that I
could solve the problem

65
00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.700
of atomicity while retaining

66
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.170
the primary key is by

67
00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.495
creating two columns
for contact numbers.

68
00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:43.410
One column for cell phones

69
00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.775
and second column for
landline numbers.

70
00:02:45.775 --> 00:02:47.180
But I still have the issue of

71
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.460
unnecessary repeated
groups of data.

72
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.000
Mary Evans is the assigned
tutor for two of the courses.

73
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.470
Her name appears twice in

74
00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.995
the table as do her
contact details.

75
00:02:56.995 --> 00:02:59.690
These instances of
data will continue to

76
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.420
reappear if she's assigned
more courses to teach.

77
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.430
It's likely that our
details will appear in

78
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.575
other tables within
a database system.

79
00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:08.360
This means I could have even

80
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.115
more groups of repeated data.

81
00:03:10.115 --> 00:03:12.020
This creates another problem.

82
00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.330
If this user changes
any of their details,

83
00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.740
then I'll have to
update their details in

84
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.170
this table and all others
in which it appears.

85
00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:19.880
If I miss any of these tables,

86
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.380
then I'll have inconsistency and

87
00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.855
invalid data within
my database system.

88
00:03:23.855 --> 00:03:26.210
To solve this issue,
I can redesign

89
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.150
my table to adhere to 1NF
or first normal form.

90
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:33.185
First, I identify the
repeating groups of data.

91
00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:36.670
In this case is the tutor's
name and contact numbers.

92
00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.890
Next, I identify the
entities I'm dealing with,

93
00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.855
which are course and tutor.

94
00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:44.300
Then I split the
course table so that

95
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.775
I now have one table
for each entity.

96
00:03:46.775 --> 00:03:48.440
A course table that contains

97
00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.255
information about the courses.

98
00:03:50.255 --> 00:03:52.160
I choose a table that maintains

99
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.920
the name and contact
numbers of each tutor.

100
00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.520
Now I need to assign a primary
key to the tutor table.

101
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.965
I select that tutor ID column.

102
00:04:00.965 --> 00:04:03.755
I've solved the problem
of data atomicity,

103
00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:07.090
but I also need to provide a
link between the two tables.

104
00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.385
I can connect the two tables
by using a foreign key.

105
00:04:10.385 --> 00:04:13.400
I just add the tutor ID
column to the course table.

106
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.515
Now both tables are linked.

107
00:04:15.515 --> 00:04:18.230
I've now achieved
data atomicity and

108
00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:21.205
eliminated unnecessary
repeating groups of data.

109
00:04:21.205 --> 00:04:24.440
You should now be familiar
with 1NF and the rules that

110
00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.230
you should apply to
avoid it. Good work.